.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt EVP_PKEY_ENCAPSULATE 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm EVP_PKEY_encapsulate_init ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_auth_encapsulate_init ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_encapsulate
.Nd Key encapsulation using a KEM algorithm with a public key
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/evp.h
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_encapsulate_init
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "const OSSL_PARAM params[]"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_auth_encapsulate_init
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "EVP_PKEY *authpriv"
.Fa "const OSSL_PARAM params[]"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_encapsulate
.Fa "EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "unsigned char *wrappedkey"
.Fa "size_t *wrappedkeylen"
.Fa "unsigned char *genkey"
.Fa "size_t *genkeylen"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn EVP_PKEY_encapsulate_init
function initializes a public key algorithm context
.Fa ctx
for an encapsulation operation and then sets the
.Em params
on the context in the same way as calling
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_params 3 .
Note that
.Fa ctx
is usually is produced using
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_new_from_pkey 3 ,
specifying the public key to use.
.Pp
The
.Fn EVP_PKEY_auth_encapsulate_init
function is similar to
.Fn EVP_PKEY_encapsulate_init
but also passes an
.Fa authpriv
authentication private key that is used during encapsulation.
.Pp
The
.Fn EVP_PKEY_encapsulate
function performs a public key encapsulation operation using
.Fa ctx .
The shared secret written to
.Fa genkey
can be used as an input for key derivation, typically for various
symmetric algorithms.
Its size is written to
.Fa genkeylen ,
which must be initialised to the size of the provided buffer.
.Pp
The ciphertext written to
.Fa wrappedkey
is an encapsulated form, which is expected to be only usable by the
holder of the private key corresponding to the public key associated
with
.Fa ctx .
This ciphertext is then communicated to the private-key holder, who can
use
.Xr EVP_PKEY_decapsulate 3
to securely recover the same shared secret.
.Pp
If
.Fa wrappedkey
is NULL then the maximum size of the output buffer is written to the
.Em *wrappedkeylen
parameter unless
.Fa wrappedkeylen
is NULL and the maximum size of the generated key buffer is written to
.Em *genkeylen
unless
.Fa genkeylen
is NULL.
.Pp
If
.Fa wrappedkey
is not NULL and the call is successful then the generated shared secret
is written to
.Fa genkey
and its size is written to
.Em *genkeylen
(which must be non-NULL). The encapsulated ciphertext is written to
.Fa wrappedkey
and its size is written to
.Em *wrappedkeylen
(must also be non-NULL), The value pointed to by
.Em wrappedlen
initially hold the size of the
.Em unwrapped
buffer so that its size can be validated by the call, ensuring it is
large enough to hold the result written to
.Em wrapped .
.Pp
Absent detailed prior knowledge of the internals of the specific KEM
algorithm, callers SHOULD NOT assume that the returned shared secret and
ciphertext are necessarily of the maximum possible length.
The lengths returned via
.Em *wrappedkeylen
and
.Em *genkeylen
SHOULD be used to determine the actual lengths of the outputs.
.Sh NOTES
After the call to
.Fn EVP_PKEY_encapsulate_init ,
algorithm-specific parameters for the operation may be set or modified
using
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_params 3 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn EVP_PKEY_encapsulate_init ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_auth_encapsulate_init
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_encapsulate
return 1 for success and 0 or a negative value for failure.
In particular a return value of -2 indicates the operation is not
supported by the public key algorithm.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Encapsulate an RSASVE key (for RSA keys).
.Bd -literal
 #include <openssl/evp.h>

 /*
  * NB: assumes rsa_pub_key is an public key of another party.
  */

 EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx = NULL;
 size_t secretlen = 0, outlen = 0;
 unsigned char *out = NULL, *secret = NULL;

 ctx = EVP_PKEY_CTX_new_from_pkey(libctx, rsa_pub_key, NULL);
 if (ctx == NULL)
     /* Error */
 if (EVP_PKEY_encapsulate_init(ctx, NULL) <= 0)
     /* Error */

 /* Set the mode - only 'RSASVE' is currently supported */
  if (EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_kem_op(ctx, "RSASVE") <= 0)
     /* Error */
 /* Determine buffer length */
 if (EVP_PKEY_encapsulate(ctx, NULL, &outlen, NULL, &secretlen) <= 0)
     /* Error */

 out = OPENSSL_malloc(outlen);
 secret = OPENSSL_malloc(secretlen);
 if (out == NULL || secret == NULL)
     /* malloc failure */

 /*
  * The generated 'secret' can be used as key material.
  * The encapsulated 'out' can be sent to another party who can
  * decapsulate it using their private key to retrieve the 'secret'.
  */
 if (EVP_PKEY_encapsulate(ctx, out, &outlen, secret, &secretlen) <= 0)
     /* Error */
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_new_from_pkey 3 ,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_decapsulate 3 ,
.Xr EVP_KEM-RSA 7 ,
.Xr EVP_KEM-X25519 7 ,
.Xr EVP_KEM-EC 7 ,
.Xr EVP_KEM-ML-KEM-512 7 ,
.Xr EVP_KEM-ML-KEM-768 7 ,
.Xr EVP_KEM-ML-KEM-1024 7
.Sh HISTORY
The functions
.Fn EVP_PKEY_encapsulate_init
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_encapsulate
were added in OpenSSL 3.0. The function
.Fn EVP_PKEY_auth_encapsulate_init
was added in OpenSSL 3.2.
.Pp
Support for
.Sy ML-KEM
was added in OpenSSL 3.5.
